{"id":4848,"date":"2019-09-08T22:36:04","date_gmt":"2019-09-09T02:36:04","guid":{"rendered":"http:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/?p=4848"},"modified":"2019-09-21T01:29:18","modified_gmt":"2019-09-21T05:29:18","slug":"dunkirk-france","status":"publish","type":"post","link":"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/travel-log\/dunkirk-france\/","title":{"rendered":"Dunkirk, France"},"content":{"rendered":"<p>Dunkirk may be infamous for Operation Dynamo during the Second World War, but it has so much more to offer. From enjoying the view of the city from the Dunkirk Belfry that was once a part of the Saint-\u00c9loi Church to the gorgeous boats lining the ports, there are many things to explore. My personal favorite was the Port Museum with its ships.<\/p>\n<div class=\"su-tabs su-tabs-style-default su-tabs-mobile-stack\" data-active=\"1\" data-scroll-offset=\"0\" data-anchor-in-url=\"no\"><div class=\"su-tabs-nav\"><span class=\"\" data-url=\"\" data-target=\"blank\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\">City Walk<\/span><span class=\"\" data-url=\"\" data-target=\"blank\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\">Port Museum<\/span><span class=\"\" data-url=\"\" data-target=\"blank\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\">The Ships<\/span><span class=\"\" data-url=\"\" data-target=\"blank\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"button\">Operation Dynamo Museum<\/span><\/div><div class=\"su-tabs-panes\"><div class=\"su-tabs-pane su-u-clearfix su-u-trim\" data-title=\"City Walk\">\n<style type=\"text\/css\">\r\n\t#foogallery-gallery-4767 .fg-image {\r\n        width: 150px;\r\n    }\r\n<\/style>\r\n\t\t\t\t<div class=\"foogallery foogallery-container foogallery-default foogallery-lightbox-foobox fg-gutter-10 fg-center fg-default fg-light fg-border-thin fg-shadow-outline fg-loading-default fg-loaded-fade-in fg-caption-hover fg-hover-fade fg-hover-zoom fg-ready\" id=\"foogallery-gallery-4767\" data-foogallery=\"{&quot;item&quot;:{&quot;showCaptionTitle&quot;:true,&quot;showCaptionDescription&quot;:true},&quot;lazy&quot;:true}\" >\r\n\t<div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/09\/20190908_110822.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"Looking from Jean Bart Square towards Town Hall (with its tower in the background), there is the belfry on the left and the Saint-\u00c9loi Church on the right. The belfry now houses the tourist center. I suggest going to the tourist center first to go up the tower and get your bearings. After you are oriented a little, you will be better able to plan your day!\" data-attachment-id=\"4773\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"Looking from Jean Bart Square towards Town Hall (with its tower in the background), there is the belfry on the left and the Saint-\u00c9loi Church on the right. The belfry now houses the tourist center. I suggest going to the tourist center first to go up the tower and get your bearings. After you are oriented a little, you will be better able to plan your day!\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2019\/09\/20190908_110822\/2731824643.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">Looking from Jean Bart Square towards Town Hall (with its tower in the background), there is the belfry on the left and the Saint-\u00c9loi Church on the right. The belfry now houses the tourist center. I suggest going to the tourist center first to go up the tower and get your bearings. After you are oriented a little, you will be better able to plan your day!<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/09\/20190908_104304.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"Dunkirk&#039;s belfry, built in around 1450, was originally the bell tower of the neighboring church. As was customary in Flemish cities, a belfry was built to be able to survey the city from up high and warn if there was a fire or enemy threat.\n&lt;p&gt;Later, as Dunkirk suffered many losses during the two world wars, there is a war memorial built into the side of the belfry. Originally intended for the First World War, it was built in 1923. The soldier&#039;s gun, hat and bag lay next to his coffin, with an inscription above reading &quot;Never forget&quot; (or more literally &#039;Remember&quot;). Above the lying soldier and his stuff is the city&#039;s coat of arms. Finally, at the top is a high relief sculpture of two women: the left representing justice and the right liberty.&lt;\/p&gt;\" data-attachment-id=\"4840\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"Dunkirk&#039;s belfry, built in around 1450, was originally the bell tower of the neighboring church. As was customary in Flemish cities, a belfry was built to be able to survey the city from up high and warn if there was a fire or enemy threat.\n&lt;p&gt;Later, as Dunkirk suffered many losses during the two world wars, there is a war memorial built into the side of the belfry. Originally intended for the First World War, it was built in 1923. The soldier&#039;s gun, hat and bag lay next to his coffin, with an inscription above reading &quot;Never forget&quot; (or more literally &#039;Remember&quot;). Above the lying soldier and his stuff is the city&#039;s coat of arms. Finally, at the top is a high relief sculpture of two women: the left representing justice and the right liberty.&lt;\/p&gt;\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2019\/09\/20190908_104304\/3971157160.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">Dunkirk's belfry, built in around 1450, was originally the bell tower of the neighboring church. As was customary in Flemish cities, a belfry was built to be able to survey the city from up high and warn if there was a fire or enemy threat.\n<p>Later, as Dunkirk suffered many losses during the two world wars, there is a war memorial built into the side of the belfry. Originally intended for the First World War, it was built in 1923. The soldier's gun, hat and bag lay next to his coffin, with an inscription above reading \"Never forget\" (or more literally 'Remember\"). Above the lying soldier and his stuff is the city's coat of arms. Finally, at the top is a high relief sculpture of two women: the left representing justice and the right liberty.<\/p><\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/09\/DSC_5005.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"At the top of the elevator, you come out at the bell level. There are fifty bells, which are all still in operation and ring every 15 minutes. (I suggest timing your visit right so that you don&#039;t end up near the bells when they ring. They are loud. Trust me.) The largest of the bells, weighing 5 tons, is named Jean Bart but more on him later. These bells are not original though as they were often melted down during times of war.\" data-attachment-id=\"4787\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"At the top of the elevator, you come out at the bell level. There are fifty bells, which are all still in operation and ring every 15 minutes. (I suggest timing your visit right so that you don&#039;t end up near the bells when they ring. They are loud. Trust me.) The largest of the bells, weighing 5 tons, is named Jean Bart but more on him later. These bells are not original though as they were often melted down during times of war.\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2019\/09\/DSC_5005\/4173309634.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">At the top of the elevator, you come out at the bell level. There are fifty bells, which are all still in operation and ring every 15 minutes. (I suggest timing your visit right so that you don't end up near the bells when they ring. They are loud. Trust me.) The largest of the bells, weighing 5 tons, is named Jean Bart but more on him later. These bells are not original though as they were often melted down during times of war.<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/09\/DSC_5006.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"There is also an enclosed box from where the bells can be played manually.\" data-attachment-id=\"4788\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"There is also an enclosed box from where the bells can be played manually.\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2019\/09\/DSC_5006\/2539584476.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">There is also an enclosed box from where the bells can be played manually.<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/09\/DSC_5011.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"After the roughly 60 steps, looking north, you can see the town hall with its belfry. To the right of the image (under one of the cranes), you can barely make out a significantly smaller tower named Leughenaer. If you walk long enough in this direction, you will get to the Netherlands.\" data-attachment-id=\"4789\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"After the roughly 60 steps, looking north, you can see the town hall with its belfry. To the right of the image (under one of the cranes), you can barely make out a significantly smaller tower named Leughenaer. If you walk long enough in this direction, you will get to the Netherlands.\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2019\/09\/DSC_5011\/792431822.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200