 went down to Chile to trade coal bought in England for the sodium nitrate desired in northern France.\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2019\/09\/DSC_5106\/2637407075.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">In the late 19th century, farmers were looking for ways to improve crop yields. Discovering that sodium nitrate could be used as a fertilizer, the farmers' demand for the product increased. As a result, many sailors went down to Chile to trade coal bought in England for the sodium nitrate desired in northern France.<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/09\/DSC_5102.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"Various inventions of the mid-19th century helped dockers deal with the increased traffic to the ports.\" data-attachment-id=\"4817\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"Various inventions of the mid-19th century helped dockers deal with the increased traffic to the ports.\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2019\/09\/DSC_5102\/1602060731.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">Various inventions of the mid-19th century helped dockers deal with the increased traffic to the ports.<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/09\/DSC_5144.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"Recently, in the late 20th century, the steel industry developed in Dunkirk. Today, it is the main activity of and the largest employer for the region.\" data-attachment-id=\"4874\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"Recently, in the late 20th century, the steel industry developed in Dunkirk. Today, it is the main activity of and the largest employer for the region.\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2019\/09\/DSC_5144\/962529624.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">Recently, in the late 20th century, the steel industry developed in Dunkirk. Today, it is the main activity of and the largest employer for the region.<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/09\/DSC_5145.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"For the steel industry, the central port, in yellow, was dug to be able to serve the larger ships. (The eastern port, in orange, was the one built in the mid-19th century to handle the extra traffic due to the Dunkirk-Paris railway. The historic port is the one created even before the city was French.)\" data-attachment-id=\"4835\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"For the steel industry, the central port, in yellow, was dug to be able to serve the larger ships. (The eastern port, in orange, was the one built in the mid-19th century to handle the extra traffic due to the Dunkirk-Paris railway. The historic port is the one created even before the city was French.)\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2019\/09\/DSC_5145\/1775709935.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">For the steel industry, the central port, in yellow, was dug to be able to serve the larger ships. (The eastern port, in orange, was the one built in the mid-19th century to handle the extra traffic due to the Dunkirk-Paris railway. The historic port is the one created even before the city was French.)<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/09\/DSC_5104.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"This is a model of the sand dunes that are present under the surface of the water near Dunkirk. As larger ships started heading towards the Dunkirk ports, many of them got caught in these dunes. In order to help these ships navigate the dunes, light ships were stationed out at sea. Go over to &quot;The Ships&quot; tab to learn more about one of those light ships.\" data-attachment-id=\"4819\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"This is a model of the sand dunes that are present under the surface of the water near Dunkirk. As larger ships started heading towards the Dunkirk ports, many of them got caught in these dunes. In order to help these ships navigate the dunes, light ships were stationed out at sea. Go over to &quot;The Ships&quot; tab to learn more about one of those light ships.\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2019\/09\/DSC_5104\/3718756489.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">This is a model of the sand dunes that are present under the surface of the water near Dunkirk. As larger ships started heading towards the Dunkirk ports, many of them got caught in these dunes. In order to help these ships navigate the dunes, light ships were stationed out at sea. Go over to \"The Ships\" tab to learn more about one of those light ships.<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><\/div>\r\n\n<\/div>\n<div class=\"su-tabs-pane su-u-clearfix su-u-trim\" data-title=\"The Ships\">\nPart of the Port Museum, there are a couple guided tours per day (in French) that are done of the two ships.<br \/>\n<style type=\"text\/css\">\r\n\t#foogallery-gallery-4845 .fg-image {\r\n        width: 150px;\r\n    }\r\n<\/style>\r\n\t\t\t\t<div class=\"foogallery foogallery-container foogallery-default foogallery-lightbox-foobox fg-gutter-10 fg-center fg-default fg-light fg-border-thin fg-shadow-outline fg-loading-default fg-loaded-fade-in fg-caption-hover fg-hover-fade fg-hover-zoom fg-ready\" id=\"foogallery-gallery-4845\" data-foogallery=\"{&quot;item&quot;:{&quot;showCaptionTitle&quot;:true,&quot;showCaptionDescription&quot;:true},&quot;lazy&quot;:true}\" >\r\n\t<div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/09\/DSC_5114.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"Located just outside the Port Museum of Dunkirk, the &lt;i&gt;Duchesse Anne&lt;\/i&gt; has been part of the Museum since 1998 (and officially opened to the public in 2001). Originally built by the Germans in 1901, the ship was used as a travelling naval school for boys until 1932, when it became stationary due to the decreased number of enrollments. The travelling naval school, then called &lt;i&gt;Duchesse Elizabeth&lt;\/i&gt;, took place in two sessions: one in the summer around Iceland and another in the winter around the equator. The 15-year-old boys would either take one year of school to become a sailor or two years to become an officer. There were 190 students on this boat during the sessions and they would follow a strict routine thanks to the powerful sound of this bell, original to this ship.\n&lt;p&gt;When the Germans lost the Second World War, they had to pay reparations to the Allies. This ship was included in the reparations payment to the United Kingdom. It was however neglected for many years and was thus in very poor condition when the city of Dunkirk purchased it in 1981. Because the restauration was extensive and because the museum wanted to reproduce it to the way it was before the war, the process took about 20 years.&lt;\/p&gt;\" data-attachment-id=\"4823\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"Located just outside the Port Museum of Dunkirk, the &lt;i&gt;Duchesse Anne&lt;\/i&gt; has been part of the Museum since 1998 (and officially opened to the public in 2001). Originally built by the Germans in 1901, the ship was used as a travelling naval school for boys until 1932, when it became stationary due to the decreased number of enrollments. The travelling naval school, then called &lt;i&gt;Duchesse Elizabeth&lt;\/i&gt;, took place in two sessions: one in the summer around Iceland and another in the winter around the equator. The 15-year-old boys would either take one year of school to become a sailor or two years to become an officer. There were 190 students on this boat during the sessions and they would follow a strict routine thanks to the powerful sound of this bell, original to this ship.\n&lt;p&gt;When the Germans lost the Second World War, they had to pay reparations to the Allies. This ship was included in the reparations payment to the United Kingdom. It was however neglected for many years and was thus in very poor condition when the city of Dunkirk purchased it in 1981. Because the restauration was extensive and because the museum wanted to reproduce it to the way it was before the war, the process took about 20 years.&lt;\/p&gt;\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2019\/09\/DSC_5114\/34847408.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">Located just outside the Port Museum of Dunkirk, the <i>Duchesse Anne<\/i> has been part of the Museum since 1998 (and officially opened to the public in 2001). Originally built by the Germans in 1901, the ship was used as a travelling naval school for boys until 1932, when it became stationary due to the decreased number of enrollments. The travelling naval school, then called <i>Duchesse Elizabeth<\/i>, took place in two sessions: one in the summer around Iceland and another in the winter around the equator. The 15-year-old boys would either take one year of school to become a sailor or two years to become an officer. There were 190 students on this boat during the sessions and they would follow a strict routine thanks to the powerful sound of this bell, original to this ship.\n<p>When the Germans lost the Second World War, they had to pay reparations to the Allies. This ship was included in the reparations payment to the United Kingdom. It was however neglected for many years and was thus in very poor condition when the city of Dunkirk purchased it in 1981. Because the restauration was extensive and because the museum wanted to reproduce it to the way it was before the war, the process took about 20 years.<\/p><\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/09\/DSC_5110.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"The small building in the middle of the deck is the kitchen. There were two cooks that prepared 600 meals per day for all of the students. Given the large amount of food that needed to be prepared and the lack of fresh food available, the food was not very nutritious and the students (like many sailors) were not in great health.\" data-attachment-id=\"4821\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"The small building in the middle of the deck is the kitchen. There were two cooks that prepared 600 meals per day for all of the students. Given the large amount of food that needed to be prepared and the lack of fresh food available, the food was not very nutritious and the students (like many sailors) were not in great health.\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2019\/09\/DSC_5110\/379479707.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">The small building in the middle of the deck is the kitchen. There were two cooks that prepared 600 meals per day for all of the students. Given the large amount of food that needed to be prepared and the lack of fresh food available, the food was not very nutritious and the students (like many sailors) were not in great health.<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/09\/DSC_5113.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"This green capstan was used to crank up the 6-ton anchor. There were often up to ten students needed to spin the capstan and pull the anchor up.\" data-attachment-id=\"4822\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"This green capstan was used to crank up the 6-ton anchor. There were often up to ten students needed to spin the capstan and pull the anchor up.\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2019\/09\/DSC_5113\/2903366113.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">This green capstan was used to crank up the 6-ton anchor. There were often up to ten students needed to spin the capstan and pull the anchor up.<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/09\/DSC_5115.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"Under the upper front deck is located the mechanical anchor crank. Under the deck are also located the four bathrooms and the two showers (for all of the 190 students!)\" data-attachment-id=\"4824\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"Under the upper front deck is located the mechanical anchor crank. Under the deck are also located the four bathrooms and the two showers (for all of the 190 students!)\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2019\/09\/DSC_5115\/22782292.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">Under the upper front deck is located the mechanical anchor crank. Under the deck are also located the four bathrooms and the two showers (for all of the 190 students!)<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/09\/DSC_5118.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"To prevent flooding the spaces under the deck, all doors had high sills. Sailors had to be very careful not to trip!\" data-attachment-id=\"4825\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"To prevent flooding the spaces under the deck, all doors had high sills. Sailors had to be very careful not to trip!\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2019\/09\/DSC_5118\/1065737933.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">To prevent flooding the spaces under the deck, all doors had high sills. Sailors had to be very careful not to trip!<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/09\/DSC_5123.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"Yet another high door sill. This door could also be closed and sealed in case of flooding. Can you see what the boys are doing in the picture on the far end?\" data-attachment-id=\"4828\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"Yet another high door sill. This door could also be closed and sealed in case of flooding. Can you see what the boys are doing in the picture on the far end?\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2019\/09\/DSC_5123\/717977202.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">Yet another high door sill. This door could also be closed and sealed in case of flooding. Can you see what the boys are doing in the picture on the far end?<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/09\/DSC_5122.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"It&#039;s laundry day! The sail cables served double duty as laundry lines.\" data-attachment-id=\"4827\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"It&#039;s laundry day! The sail cables served double duty as laundry lines.\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2019\/09\/DSC_5122\/118434833.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">It's laundry day! The sail cables served double duty as laundry lines.<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/09\/DSC_5121.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"The large open spaces in the room before and in this room served as both a cafeteria and a dormitory. Every night the tables would be hoisted up to the ceiling and the hammocks would be placed on the hooks. The hammocks were typically a lot higher and closer to the ceiling, as they would swing less when they had less rope.\" data-attachment-id=\"4826\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"The large open spaces in the room before and in this room served as both a cafeteria and a dormitory. Every night the tables would be hoisted up to the ceiling and the hammocks would be placed on the hooks. The hammocks were typically a lot higher and closer to the ceiling, as they would swing less when they had less rope.\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2019\/09\/DSC_5121\/744178106.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">The large open spaces in the room before and in this room served as both a cafeteria and a dormitory. Every night the tables would be hoisted up to the ceiling and the hammocks would be placed on the hooks. The hammocks were typically a lot higher and closer to the ceiling, as they would swing less when they had less rope.<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/09\/DSC_5125.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"A typical day for a sailor student consisted of class (think history, math, science, etc.) in the morning and naval studies in the afternoon. On calm days however, the students would get some time off. They would either play board or card games or they would learn how to play an instrument. Playing an instrument was rather important as a sailor as that would mean a higher salary in the future.\" data-attachment-id=\"4851\" class=\"fg-thumb\"><span class=\"fg-image-wrap\"><img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" title=\"A typical day for a sailor student consisted of class (think history, math, science, etc.) in the morning and naval studies in the afternoon. On calm days however, the students would get some time off. They would either play board or card games or they would learn how to play an instrument. Playing an instrument was rather important as a sailor as that would mean a higher salary in the future.\" width=\"150\" height=\"150\" class=\"skip-lazy fg-image\" data-src-fg=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/cache\/2019\/09\/DSC_5125\/3622579715.jpg\" src=\"data:image\/svg+xml,%3Csvg%20xmlns%3D%22http%3A%2F%2Fwww.w3.org%2F2000%2Fsvg%22%20width%3D%22150%22%20height%3D%22150%22%20viewBox%3D%220%200%20150%20150%22%3E%3C%2Fsvg%3E\"><\/span><span class=\"fg-image-overlay\"><\/span><\/a><figcaption class=\"fg-caption\"><div class=\"fg-caption-inner\"><div class=\"fg-caption-title\">A typical day for a sailor student consisted of class (think history, math, science, etc.) in the morning and naval studies in the afternoon. On calm days however, the students would get some time off. They would either play board or card games or they would learn how to play an instrument. Playing an instrument was rather important as a sailor as that would mean a higher salary in the future.<\/div><\/div><\/figcaption><\/figure><div class=\"fg-loader\"><\/div><\/div><div class=\"fg-item fg-type-image fg-idle\"><figure class=\"fg-item-inner\"><a href=\"https:\/\/iboishin.github.io/portfolio-website\/wp-content\/uploads\/2019\/09\/20190908_154130.jpg\" data-caption-title=\"The sub-officers benefited from a little more comfort, having their own room with a bed and a dresser. Another important difference is the fact that the officer quarters were lined with wood. This not only made for a more elegant space but it also preserved some of the warmth in the room.\" data-att